[00:00.00]Proposition three is about whether existing higher income-tax rates
[00:04.40]should expire.
[00:06.10]Voters approved these rates in twenty twelve and extended
[00:09.08]them in twenty sixteen.
[00:11.18]They are now scheduled to end after twenty thirty.
[00:14.48]The additional rates apply to roughly the highest-earning two
[00:17.27]percent of taxpayers.
[00:19.40]This is a tax on income,
[00:21.36]such as wages or investment profits,
[00:24.08]rather than a tax on everything someone owns.
[00:27.52]Proposition three would make those higher rates permanent.
[00:31.30]It would keep the current system beyond its scheduled
[00:33.74]expiration,
[00:34.98]rather than introducing a new increase in everybody’s rate
[00:38.32]today.
[00:39.33]Imagine a teacher planning future classes and support services.
[00:43.75]If an existing revenue stream ends,
[00:46.13]a budget planner must consider cuts, replacement taxes,
[00:49.99]or other funding.
[00:51.91]Keeping that revenue can avoid that particular gap, but
[00:54.93]does not promise any school a specific staffing level.
[00:58.67]School budgets depend on the wider state budget.
[01:01.80]The analyst estimates five to fifteen billion dollars of
[01:05.30]annual revenue maintained after the scheduled expiration.
[01:09.46]The wide range reflects the volatile incomes and investment
[01:12.90]gains of high earners.
[01:15.18]It is not a guaranteed fifteen billion every year.
[01:18.82]Roughly forty percent
[01:19.98]would support schools and community colleges.
[01:23.04]The remainder supports other state programs and reserves.
[01:26.68]Those shares describe the analyst’s estimate, rather than a
